speechtext:
Why should we complain if they exercise all the rights that the agreement contains, and say to them: We are going to take your property from you because you have the right to appeal. Their answer is: "Sir, that is what we contracted to have. You have no right to complain. All we ask is fulfilment of the agreement. We ask it in this case not' only touching the appeal, but we say your own agreement contemplated that you

should give us an extension of time. You evidently contemplated you were not going to complete this arbitration in nine months, and you make provision for extension by Order in Council." I am putting myself in the position of the English shareholder. He will be able to say, with force, I am afraid: "All we asked was what the agreement contemplated by the extension. We asked for three months' extension. There is the agreement which contemplated that we might have an extension. But your Government says: No, we will only give you that three months' extension if you give us possession of the property at once." My hon. friend for North Oxford (Mr. Nesbitt) argues that it is of vital importance to have the property. Is it vitally different whether you get the property in nine months or in twelve months? An argument of that nature would have applied very well to taking the property in the beginning. If, instead of making arrangements, the Government had provided by their agreement to take possession of the Grand Trunk at once, it would have been the more logical position. They could have said: We have arranged the finances, and you will get your money; meanwhile we get possession. It would have been a stronger argument then than to-day, when you contemplate leaving the system in their hands until the arbitration is completed. Again I repeat, I am not saying this in any adverse spirit;
I am trying to look at the position as would the English shareholder-if he is reasonable; he is not always reasonable; many of the English financial newspapers are unreasonable; and when a shareholder of a Canadian company takes his grievance to them they magnify it to the disadvantage of Canada. So long as we have a good case I am content, but I am afraid the English shareholders can make a stronger case on this particular matter than I would like them to have.
